      "text": "   Let $a = x + \\frac{1}{x}$. Then, we have $x^2 + \\frac{1}{x^2} = \\left( x + \\frac{1}{x} \\right)^2 - 2 = a^2 - 2$. \n\n   Given that $a^3 - 2 = (x + \\frac{1}{x})^3 - 2 = 4^3 - 2 = 64 - 2 = 62$, we get $a^3 - 2 = 62 \\implies a^3 = 64$, and thus $a = 4$. \n\nTherefore, \n   $x^3 - \\frac{1}{x^3} = (x - \\frac{1}{x})(x^2 + \\frac{1}{x^2} + 1) = 4(62 + 1) = \\(\\boxed{248}\\)$.",
